This pull request adds 2 new classes to the `data_source.py` and `species.py` schemas namely `DataSourceInDBIDOnly`, `DataSourceIDOnly` and `SpeciesIDOnlyInDB`, `SpeciesID` respectively. Now the API endpoint `/api/v1/data_source/` will serve data in the format as specified below.

`[
  {
    "id": 0,
    "title": "string",
    "species": [
      {
        "id": "string",
        "data_source_id": 0
      }
    ]
  }
]`

The following function would cause Prusti to crash:

```rust
fn prepare_client(client_state: &AnyClientState) -> Result<u32, u64> {
    let r = match client_state {
        AnyClientState::Tendermint(cs) => cs,
        _ =>  return Err(0)
    };
    panic!()
}
```

I think the reason is that the behaviour of `encode_block_statements` is to not encode the statements if the block ends in `panic!()`. However, that doesn't seem sound, considering it is possible for the control flow to return explicitly with a `return` statement (in fact the `panic!()` may never be reachable).

This removes the panic check and always encodes the statements instead.

resolves #612

Changes for custom input converter support.  Allows customers to plugin their converter implementation to the pipeline to customize how function input parameters are populated.

Major changes:

1. Exposed an `InputConverters` property to WorkerOptions. Customers will use this property to add their custom converters when bootstrapping the app.

2. Added a new `InputConversionFeature`. This feature is responsible for finding the appropriate converter to be used and does the conversion. This feature uses the 7 built-in converters along with any other custom converters customer has defined.

3. The existing `DefaultBindingFeature` does not directly work with the converters now. Instead, it uses the new `InputConversionFeature` implementation to do the conversion.


#### How does function app developers use this?
Customers can create a new custom input converter by implementing the `IInputConverter` interface.

    public interface IInputConverter
    {
        ValueTask<ConversionResult> ConvertAsync(ConverterContext context);
    }
The `ConverterContext` instance will have information such as target type and source data (input data/ trigger data).

The custom converter can be hooked up to the function app in any of the following 3 approaches.

### 1) App level
Add the new converter to `WorkerOptions.InputConverters` while bootstrapping the app.

```
var host = new HostBuilder()
            .ConfigureFunctionsWorkerDefaults((workerOptions) =>
            {
                // Add MyCustomConverter to the pipeline
                workeroptions.InputConverters.Register<MyCustomConverter>();
            })
            .Build();

await host.RunAsync();
```
This option also allows the customers to insert this converter in a specific position or clear all existing built-in converters etc.

##### Adding at a specific index:
````
// Adds MyCustomConverter to the set of available (built-in) converters, but at index 1. 
workeroptions.InputConverters.RegisterAt<MyCustomConverter>(1);
````
##### Clearing existing registered(7 built-in) converters and adding custom converters
````
// Adds MyCustomConverter to the set of available (built-in) converters, but at index 1. 
workeroptions.InputConverters.Clear();
workeroptions.InputConverters.Register<MyCustomConverter>();
workeroptions.InputConverters.Register<MyOtherCustomConverter>();
````


### 2) Type level
Decorate the type used as the parameter of the function with `InputConverter` attribute where you will define the custom converter type to be used.

```
[InputConverter(typeof(MyCustomConverter))]
public sealed class Product
{
    public int Id { set; get; }
    public string Name { set; get; }
}
```
and

```
[Function("products")]
public HttpResponseData Run(
                 [HttpTrigger(AuthorizationLevel.Anonymous, "get", Route = null)] HttpRequestData req,
                 Product product)
{
   ....    
}
```

### 3) Function parameter level
Decorate the function parameter using the `InputConverter` attribute

```
[Function("products")]
public HttpResponseData Run(
                 [HttpTrigger(AuthorizationLevel.Anonymous, "get", Route = null)] HttpRequestData req,
                 [InputConverter(typeof(MyCustomConverter))] ProductViewModel productvm)
{
   ....    
}
```
